A||||7||ChadMan||Fixing A/C
Z||000000||ChadMan||07-22-99||12:23 PM||||My '86 V6 SE had been sitting in storage for almost two years before I bought it. It has A/C, but there was no belt on the compressor.  I installed a belt and to my amazement the A/C worked great for two months.  Then the compressor seized up tight and broke the belt.<p>I have a friend who will recharge my A/C with R12 for $60.  I found a good compressor for $50, but haven't bought it yet.  What do you guys think?  Is there any reason why I shouldn't just vent the system, install the new compressor, and have my buddy recharge the system?  Or is there some reason why it's likely to fail again soon?  Any input is appreciated (unless you're a tree-hugger telling me I'm one step up from Hitler).  Thanks!
Z||000001||theogre||07-22-99||01:07 PM||||Venting R12 is a felony in the U.S. If your friend is handling R12 w/o certification and/or proper equipment, that is also a felony class crime. Large Fines and jail time...... EPA pays rewards for tips..... <p>Have someone with the proper tools remove the freon. It's usually not expensive. You may as well have it leak tested to make sure only the compressor is shot.
Z||000002||Carrolles||07-22-99||04:43 PM||||If your compressor locked up you should also replace the dryer and clean and flush the system since it likely has shavings from the fragged compressor in it.<br>
Z||000003||RICK||07-22-99||05:46 PM||||Chadman, I'm not a tree-hugger but but I have to agree with org, check out a local trade college that has a heating / A/C class, they might pull out the R-12 for you and not charge you a thing we would at the tech-college I work at.
Z||000004||Cooter||07-22-99||07:55 PM||||Speaking of removing the r12 from your AC- I found out that is what happened to the freon in my fiero. It was a "back row special" at the car lot and the air worked so they "recycled" the freon out of it and put it into a "front row car" that needed it. That stinks!
Z||000005||ChadMan||07-23-99||07:38 AM||||Thanks for the advice!  I will definitely check around now to see if any local shops/trade schools will reclaim my R-12 for free.<p>Carrolles, how much do you think a new dryer would run me?  And how much would it cost to have the system cleaned and flushed?  Maybe I should ask whoever reclaims the R-12 to do it.  2 lbs of R-12 is pretty valuable!
Z||000006||Speadraser||07-28-99||11:06 PM||||before you recharge your system w/ the new compressor replace the 5/8 I.D. flex hose that runs from the drier down under the car.  Its about 14 inches long and the "barrier hose runs about $3-$4 a foot and a dollar for each clamp and it will save you having to purge the system again to fix the leak.  I understand almost all fieros leak at this location at one time or another mine did at 88,000 miles.  Be careful not to damage the barbs on the hardlines when cutting away the old hose.  BTW you'll have to get the hose from a A/C shop and a drier will cost you about $40-$65.
Z||000007||Carrolles||07-30-99||12:04 PM||||I bought a dryer at AutoZone for $55.00 US.  you can flush the system yourself.  Buy the flush fluid at an autp parts store and blow in through the refrigerant lines with an air compressor.<p>Good luck,<br>Carroll
